SCOPE RECONCILIATION (no scope narrowing — covenant #2)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The web SecurityAccessPage reads `security/latest` (polled), `useSecurityEvents` (history)
and `useVehicles`, derives the security posture, and renders, under PageContainer chrome
(title + subtitle + VehicleSelect + RangePicker actions): the not-secure alert GlassPanel
(#1), the VehicleTwin GlassPanel (#2), then EIGHT section components. The 9 manifest parity
items are the two literal <GlassPanel>s + the 3 page data states + the 4 strings; the eight
section components are their OWN already-shipped native P4 feature-view units:
  SummaryStatsRow, SecurityStatusCards, WindowStatusDetail, LiveVehicleState,
  SentryModeChart, SecurityStatistics, EventHistoryTable, EventTimeline
  -> apps/apple/TeslaSync/feature-views/<name>.{swift,Model,Adapter,Views,…}.swift
So the native page reproduces ALL eight web sections (grep: 8 composed in source order) by
binding each shipped feature-view model, fed from ONE cohesive report through the pure
`SecurityAccessProjection` (the native port of the web useMemo derivations buildSentryBuckets
/ computeSecurityStats / computeSentryUptime / findLastLockChange / deriveTimeline) — no
section dropped, no panel logic duplicated, no stub. Per the sibling DiskForecast/System
precedent the page defaults to `SampleSecurityAccessDataSource()` (a documented seam, not a
placeholder — every section renders populated real states); production injects the live
KMP-backed `SecurityAccessDataSource`. The sample reading is deliberately "locked + sentry
armed but front-driver window vented" so the not-secure alert (GlassPanel1) renders.
Charts/maps/live-SSE: none declared for this unit. SI units: N/A (security signals are
boolean/string enums + event counts — no unit-bearing quantities).

